WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.220
Dangling access happens when code keeps a way to reach an object after that object is gone, moved from in a way that invalidates the expectation, or otherwise no longer valid for the operation.

2
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:20.200
A dangling reference, a stale raw pointer, or a stored view can look harmless because the type still compiles and the address may still contain data.

3
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.020
The defect is that the program no longer has a valid object behind the access.

4
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.940
Common patterns deserve special review.

5
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.680
Do not return references or pointers to local objects.

6
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.580
Be careful with references bound to temporaries.

7
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.960
Treat moved-from objects according to their documented state, not according to what happened in one test run.

8
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:48.340
When containers change, understand which operations invalidate iterators, references, and pointers.

9
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:55.240
A vector reallocation, an erase operation, or a string modification can make previously saved access unsafe.

10
00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:03.980
The right response is not to memorize every rule in isolation, but to design code so validity is checked close to use and long-lived aliases are avoided.

11
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.580
Asynchronous work adds another layer.

12
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.440
A callback, task, or event handler may run after the object it captured has been destroyed.

13
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:21.100
Capturing this, storing references in lambdas, or retaining spans across scheduling boundaries should trigger lifetime questions during review.

14
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:29.680
Prefer ownership models that make the lifetime explicit, cancellation paths that are clear, and tests that exercise object teardown and boundary timing.

15
00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:38.260
Preventing use-after-free risk is mainly about making stale access difficult to create and easy to spot before it becomes a production defect.

16
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:51.100
APIs can help by returning values instead of references when lifetime would be ambiguous, by documenting invalidation behavior near mutating operations, and by keeping views close to the data source.

17
00:01:51.200 --> 00:02:02.080
Tests should include erase, reallocation, move, shutdown, and cancellation paths, because those are the paths where stale access often becomes visible.
